# Task: Generate board_ate_p4 — Lightweight Board Description Layer

## Platform
- ESP-IDF: v5.5.3
- Chip: ESP32-P4
- Language: C (C17)
- Style reference: Espressif official BSP conventions

---

## Design philosophy (read carefully before generating any code)

This is a **Board Description Layer**, NOT a full BSP business layer.

The board layer serves one purpose:
> Centralize all hardware-default descriptions (GPIO, bus numbers, addresses,
> baud rates, capabilities) so engineers can modify board_ate_p4.h by reading
> the schematic — without touching Driver, System, or App code.

Espressif's official BSP pattern (reference: esp32_p4_function_ev_board, esp-box-3, etc.)
follows this sequence:
1. Define pinout macros  (BSP_I2C_*, BSP_LCD_*, BSP_USB_*, ...)
2. Define capability macros  (BSP_CAPS_DISPLAY, BSP_CAPS_TOUCH, ...)
3. Define thin API  (bsp_i2c_init(), bsp_i2c_get_handle(), ...)

This project uses `BOARD_` prefix instead of `BSP_` because it is a
project-specific board component, not a redistributable Espressif BSP.

---

## Layer responsibilities (do NOT violate these boundaries)

| Layer      | Prefix       | What it describes                                      |
|------------|--------------|--------------------------------------------------------|
| Board      | `BOARD_`     | How this PCB is wired — GPIO, bus numbers, addresses   |
| Driver     | `driver_`    | How a peripheral device is operated                    |
| System     | `system_`    | How capabilities are orchestrated into services        |
| App        | `app_`       | What business actions the user performs                |

The board layer must NOT contain:
- Instrument scan logic
- Module scan logic
- Protocol parsing
- Event system registration
- Routing logic
- FreeRTOS task creation
- driver_create() / driver_start() calls
- Business state machines
- UI page logic

---

